// cc-candybar: multi-color-git.json5
//
// Reference pattern for per-field git segment coloring (brandon-segments-3eo):
// each fact in the git segment — branch, staged/unstaged/untracked counts,
// conflicts — gets its OWN semantic palette color instead of one uniform
// segment `fg`, so a dirty tree reads at a glance without parsing the text
// (the p10k / gitaculous-style terminal-prompt convention). This is what the
// bundled default's `git`/`gitaculous` segment templates
// (src/config/default-dsl-config.ts) already do; this file lifts that same
// template body out into a standalone, heavily-annotated example so a
// template author can copy the pattern without reading the DEFAULT_DSL_CONFIG
// source.
//
// ── The two ideas worth taking from this file ──────────────────────────────
//
// 1. COLOR BY NAME, never by hardcoded value. `{{ color "primary" }}` resolves
//    against whichever theme, look and hue shift are active for this segment,
//    so the same template stays legible across every theme a user picks. A
//    literal `#ff0000` would not.
//
// 2. QUIET IS THE DEFAULT, not a wrapper. The segment's `fg:` is a *computed*
//    color, so structural text — the `⎇` glyph, the parentheses, the spaces —
//    recedes without the template saying anything about it, and only the
//    operative facts name a color. That inversion is why the template below
//    contains no de-emphasis markup at all.
//
// ── The colour vocabulary ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A colour is a value. `color` produces one from a palette name (or passes a
// `#RRGGBB` literal through); the math functions transform one; `fg`/`bg`
// paint one onto text. They compose by nesting, so the simple case is a prefix
// of the composed case — you add an adjustment by WRAPPING working code, never
// by rewriting it into a different form:
//
//   {{ fg (color "primary") .git.branch }}
//   {{ fg (darken (color "primary") 2) .git.branch }}
//   {{ fg (mix (color "primary") (bgOf) 40) .git.branch }}
//
// Available on any colour: darken/lighten (HSL levels), mix (blend a→b by
// percent), contrastOn (black or white, whichever is readable), readableOn
// (keep the colour, guarantee a WCAG ratio), and the four OKLCH axes
// (shiftHue, scaleChroma, scaleLightness, shiftLightness).
//
// `bgOf` is the current segment's own resolved background — the thing to blend
// *toward* when you want text to recede. Use it rather than the theme's
// `background`: this segment sits on `surface-active`, so blending toward
// `background` would mute toward a colour that is not actually behind it.
//
// Try it (static demo, no click state needed — this example has no
// interactive elements):
//   pnpm demo examples/multi-color-git.json5
// or point a real config at it:
//   cp examples/multi-color-git.json5 ~/.config/cc-candybar/config.json5
//
// Everything else merges on top of DEFAULT_DSL_CONFIG; only `segments.git`
// and `root` are declared here.
{
  segments: {
    git: {
      // Five distinct palette names, one per fact:
      //   primary  — the branch name (the identity of the segment)
      //   success  — staged files (green-family: ready to commit) AND the
      //              ahead count (green-family: unpushed local work)
      //   warning  — unstaged files AND the behind count (both mean
      //              "needs attention" — uncommitted edits / needs a pull)
      //   accent   — untracked files (new, not yet in git's index at all —
      //              distinct from "unstaged", which implies tracked+edited)
      //   error    — merge conflicts (the one state that blocks a commit)
      // Everything NOT wrapped in an `fg` — the ⎇ glyph, the parentheses, the
      // separating spaces — inherits the quiet `fg:` below.
      // Compare this to the bundled default's GIT_TEMPLATE/GIT_WORKTREE in
      // src/config/default-dsl-config.ts — same coloring convention, just
      // inlined here instead of factored through shared TS template-string
      // constants (this file has no TS module to factor into).
      template: '⎇ {{ fg (color "primary") .git.branch }}{{ if or (gt .git.ahead 0) (gt .git.behind 0) }} {{ if gt .git.ahead 0 }}{{ fg (color "success") (printf "↑%v" .git.ahead) }}{{ end }}{{ if gt .git.behind 0 }}{{ fg (color "warning") (printf "↓%v" .git.behind) }}{{ end }}{{ end }}{{ if or (gt .git.staged 0) (gt .git.unstaged 0) (gt .git.untracked 0) (gt .git.conflicts 0) }} ({{ $first := true }}{{ if gt .git.staged 0 }}{{ fg (color "success") (printf "+%v" .git.staged) }}{{ $first = false }}{{ end }}{{ if gt .git.unstaged 0 }}{{ if not $first }} {{ end }}{{ fg (color "warning") (printf "~%v" .git.unstaged) }}{{ $first = false }}{{ end }}{{ if gt .git.untracked 0 }}{{ if not $first }} {{ end }}{{ fg (color "accent") (printf "?%v" .git.untracked) }}{{ $first = false }}{{ end }}{{ if gt .git.conflicts 0 }}{{ if not $first }} {{ end }}{{ fg (color "error") (printf "!%v" .git.conflicts) }}{{ $first = false }}{{ end }}){{ end }}',
      bg: "surface-active",
      // A computed `fg:`. The field is a template that evaluates to a colour,
      // and `bgOf` is available here because a segment's background resolves
      // BEFORE its foreground. Read it as: blend the theme's foreground 60% of
      // the way toward this segment's background, but never past a 3:1 contrast
      // ratio (WCAG's large-text floor).
      //
      // The floor is what makes this theme-independent. A bare 60% blend
      // measures anywhere from 1.9:1 to 3.1:1 depending on how far a given
      // theme's foreground sits from its surface — legible in some themes,
      // genuinely hard to read in others. Flooring it lands every theme at
      // ~3:1: as quiet as that theme can afford, and never quieter.
      fg: '{{ readableOn (mix (color "foreground") (bgOf) 60) (bgOf) 3 }}',
      when: '{{ ne .git.branch "" }}',
    },
  },

  // A minimal, focused row — just the segment this example is about.
  root: { h: ["directory", "git"] },
}
